counterfactuals.output:
An invention is provided for improving the output characteristics and cycle lifespan of a lithium-ion secondary battery by using nickel-manganese-cobalt composite oxide particles having a controlled porous structure and optimized surface area.

The invention relates to a positive-electrode active material comprising lithium-containing nickel-manganese-cobalt composite oxide particles, for example LiNixMnyCozO₂, where x, y, and z are selected to balance high capacity, thermal stability, and durability. The particles are engineered to have internal and/or surface pores of controlled size and distribution, together with a specific surface area within a predetermined range.

By controlling porosity, the composite oxide particles allow easier penetration of the electrolyte into the electrode and shorten lithium-ion diffusion pathways during charge and discharge. This reduces internal resistance and improves high-rate discharge output. At the same time, the surface area is controlled so that it is large enough to enhance reaction kinetics but not so large that excessive side reactions occur between the active material and electrolyte. As a result, gas generation, transition-metal dissolution, and surface degradation are suppressed, thereby extending battery cycle life.

The particles may be secondary particles formed by aggregation of primary particles. The secondary particles can include interconnected pores or voids that function as electrolyte channels. The porosity may be adjusted during precursor synthesis, heat treatment, atmosphere control, particle growth, or use of pore-forming agents. The final calcined lithium composite oxide preferably has a uniform particle-size distribution, stable crystal structure, and optimized tap density for electrode fabrication.

A lithium-ion battery using this positive-electrode active material exhibits:

- improved power output, especially under high-current discharge;
- reduced polarization and lower internal resistance;
- enhanced electrolyte accessibility within the electrode;
- improved capacity retention after repeated charge-discharge cycles;
- reduced cracking or structural collapse of active-material particles; and
- improved balance between energy density, rate capability, and lifespan.

The invention may further include coating or surface treatment of the porous nickel-manganese-cobalt composite oxide particles with a protective oxide, phosphate, fluoride, carbonaceous layer, or other stabilizing material to further reduce parasitic reactions and improve thermal and electrochemical stability.

Thus, the invention improves lithium-ion battery performance by precisely controlling the morphology of NMC-based composite oxide particles, especially their pore structure and specific surface area, to achieve both high output and long service life.
